    "evaluator_feedback": "```\n[DIAGNOSIS]:\nThe root cause of the syntax error at line 351 is the use of the quantifier \"one\" combined with a set variable (\"as: set Administrator\")—specifically, the fragment \"one as: set Administrator | #as = 2\". In Alloy 6, the \"one\" quantifier introduces exactly one atom, not a subset or set, and cannot be used to bind set variables. Previous failed fixes attempted to use \"some as: set Administrator | #as = 2\" or to enumerate disjoint pairs, but neither is valid for expressing \"exactly two admins activate\" unless there is a predicate indicating what it means for an admin to participate in activation. The real issue is that the assertion lacks a precise connection to the transition—there is no relation or predicate being asserted about the admins and the mode change. The quantifier is attempting to assert something about a set of admins, but this is not well-formed Alloy: quantifiers in Alloy range over atoms, not sets.\n\n[FIX INTENT]:\nReplace the invalid quantified set expression with a direct cardinality constraint on Administrator, scoped to the transition.\n\n[REPAIR INSTRUCTIONS]:\n- Step 1: Delete the line \"one as: set Administrator | #as = 2\" from the assertion assertEmergencyActivationExactlyTwoAdmins.\n- Step 2: Replace it with a direct constraint within the implication that states: \"#Administrator = 2\".\n  So, the assertion body becomes:\n      all s, s2: State |\n          s.mode = Normal and s2.mode = Emergency and Next[s, s2] =>\n              #Administrator = 2\n\n[RATIONALE]:\nThis fix is Alloy 6 compliant because it avoids illegal set quantification entirely and instead asserts directly that in any valid transition from Normal to Emergency, the model must have exactly two administrators; it is also a different strategy from failed attempts, which all tried to quantify over sets or pairs.\n\n[LESSON]:\nWhen Alloy quantifiers cannot bind sets, express set cardinalities using direct cardinality constraints rather than quantified set variables.\n```",
