    "evaluator_feedback": "```\n[DIAGNOSIS]:\nThe syntax error at line 259 arises from the incorrect use of a type annotation within a set comprehension in Alloy 6. The code `{d.role: Role | ...}` is invalid because Alloy 6 set comprehensions do not allow type annotations (`: Role`) after an expression. The correct form is `{e | ...}` or `{e.f | ...}` for an expression, but not `{e.f: Type | ...}`. The use of `: Role` after `d.role` is the root cause of the syntax error.\n\n[FIX INTENT]:\nRemove the invalid type annotation `: Role` from the set comprehension in the function body.\n\n[REPAIR INSTRUCTIONS]:\n- Step 1: Locate the function directRoles at lines 258–260.\n- Step 2: In line 259, replace `{d.role: Role | some d: Delegation | d.to = u and d in s.activeDelegations and d.role = d.role}` with `{d.role | some d: Delegation | d.to = u and d in s.activeDelegations and d.role = d.role}` (i.e., remove `: Role` after `d.role`).\n- Step 3: Ensure that the set comprehension now reads: `{d.role | some d: Delegation | d.to = u and d in s.activeDelegations and d.role = d.role}`.\n\n[RATIONALE]:\nThis fix is Alloy 6 compliant because it adheres to the set comprehension syntax, which does not permit type annotations within the curly braces; this approach is different from failed attempts as it directly removes the unsupported syntax rather than restructuring the comprehension or changing scoping.\n\n[LESSON]:\nIn Alloy 6, type annotations are not allowed inside set comprehensions; only variable bindings and expressions should be used.\n```",
