    "evaluator_feedback": "```\n[DIAGNOSIS]:\nThe syntax error at line 259, column 77 is due to the incorrect usage of comprehension syntax inside a set subtraction operation. In Alloy 6, comprehensions like {d: Delegation | ... | ...} are not valid; instead, set comprehensions must take the form {expr : type | predicate}, and they cannot use a '|' to project a field directly after the predicate. Here, the code attempts to subtract a set of roles, but the comprehension does not actually return a set of Role atoms—it returns a set of Delegation atoms (or is malformed). The intent appears to be: from s.userRoles[u], subtract all roles that are currently held via delegation to user u in s.activeDelegations. The expression after '-' must yield a set of Role, so the comprehension must be rewritten to properly collect all such roles, using a set comprehension or relational image.\n\n[FIX INTENT]:\nRewrite the set subtraction expression so that the right-hand side is a set of Role atoms, properly collecting the roles delegated to user u in s.activeDelegations.\n\n[REPAIR INSTRUCTIONS]:\n- Step 1: Replace the entire comprehension {d: Delegation | d.to = u and d in s.activeDelegations | d.role} with the Alloy 6-compliant expression: s.activeDelegations.(to = u).role, or, more clearly, with the set comprehension {d: Delegation | d.to = u and d in s.activeDelegations}.role.\n- Step 2: The revised function should read:\n  fun directRoles[s: State, u: User]: set Role {\n      s.userRoles[u] - {d: Delegation | d.to = u and d in s.activeDelegations}.role\n  }\n\n[RATIONALE]:\nThis fix leverages Alloy 6's set comprehension and dot-join semantics to produce a set of Role atoms, matching the expected type, and avoids the invalid use of '|' for projection inside comprehensions, thus resolving the syntax error with a different strategy than the previously failed attempt.\n\n[LESSON]:\nWhen subtracting sets in Alloy, ensure both operands are of the correct type (here, set Role), and use dot-join after a set comprehension to extract related fields, not a second '|'.\n```",
