    "evaluator_feedback": "```\n=== NEXT ACTION DECISION ===\nDecision: refine/narrow fix\nRationale: Model constraints in DelegationMutex are still too restrictive in Emergency mode, preventing the required scenario for mutex roles via delegation; previous attempts did not sufficiently adjust the fact, so a targeted refinement is needed.\n\n=== VERIFICATION STATUS ===\n- Syntax: OK\n- Baseline: Satisfiable\n- Combined Model: Satisfiable\n- Assertions: Counterexamples (assertMutexAllowedDuringEmergency fails)\n- Instances: Meaningful (except for the mutex relaxation case)\n- Vacuity: None\n\n=== REPAIR INSTRUCTIONS ===\nFocus on the DelegationMutex fact:\n- In the Emergency mode branch, the intention is to allow a user to hold both members of a mutex pair only if at least one is held via delegation (and not both by direct assignment).\n- However, the model may not be constructing such a state because the logic does not actively enable holding both via delegation, it only fails to prohibit it.\n- Refine the DelegationMutex fact to explicitly allow (and not just fail to block) the holding of both mutex roles via delegation in Emergency mode:\n    - In the Emergency mode clause, after ensuring not both are held via direct assignment, explicitly allow the case where both are held via delegation, or one direct/one delegated.\n    - Consider splitting the logic for Emergency mode into:\n        a) Forbid both via direct assignment.\n        b) Require that if both mutex roles are held, at least one is held via a current delegation in activeDelegations.\n    - Additionally, review the initialization and transition facts to ensure that, in Emergency mode, delegations can be granted such that a user may actually come to hold both mutex roles (with at least one via delegation).\n- Example adjustment:\n    - In the Emergency branch, instead of:\n      ```\n      (r1 in s.userRoles[u] and r2 in s.userRoles[u]) =>\n          (\n              (some d1: Delegation | d1.to = u and d1.role = r1 and d1 in s.activeDelegations)\n              or\n              (some d2: Delegation | d2.to = u and d2.role = r2 and d2 in s.activeDelegations)\n          )\n          and not (r1 in directRoles[s, u] and r2 in directRoles[s, u])\n      ```\n      try explicitly allowing:\n      ```\n      (r1 in s.userRoles[u] and r2 in s.userRoles[u]) =>\n          (\n              (r1 in directRoles[s, u] and some d2: Delegation | d2.to = u and d2.role = r2 and d2 in s.activeDelegations)\n              or\n              (r2 in directRoles[s, u] and some d1: Delegation | d1.to = u and d1.role = r1 and d1 in s.activeDelegations)\n              or\n              (some d1: Delegation | d1.to = u and d1.role = r1 and d1 in s.activeDelegations and\n                   some d2: Delegation | d2.to = u and d2.role = r2 and d2 in s.activeDelegations)\n          )\n      ```\n      and separately ensure `not (r1 in directRoles[s, u] and r2 in directRoles[s, u])`.\n- After this, rerun the assertion and relevant scenario predicate.\n- If still unsat, check that the Delegation relation and its instantiation/scoping are not constrained elsewhere (e.g., are enough Delegation atoms available, and are the run/check scopes sufficient to allow multiple delegations per user?).\n\n=== ASSUMPTIONS REVIEW ===\n- Assumption: That delegations can be created (in Emergency) such that a user may acquire both members of a mutex pair, with at least one via delegation.\n- Category: confirm\n- Rationale: The requirements intend for this to be possible, but the model may not actually construct such a scenario due to missing delegation creation transitions or overconstrained delegation facts. Confirm that the requirements truly intend for such dynamic delegation in Emergency mode.\n\n=== REQUIREMENT UPDATES ===\nNone - requirements are clear\n\n=== UPDATED USER QUESTIONS ===\nNone\n\n=== Q&A STATUS UPDATE ===\nNone - no status changes\n\n=== HANDOFF TO RE ===\nPriority 1 (Critical blockers): Refine the DelegationMutex fact in Emergency mode to actively allow a user to hold both mutex roles, provided at least one is via delegation and neither is held solely via direct assignment; verify delegation creation supports this scenario.\nPriority 2 (Important improvements): Confirm that delegation transitions and scoping permit multiple delegations to a user for different roles in Emergency mode.\nPriority 3 (Optional enhancements): None\n```",
