entClearance, DelegationConstraints, etc.) prevents this pattern even in Emergency mode, and explicitly allow the constructive witness.\n- Example: You may need to explicitly allow (in Emergency mode) that if a user receives a delegation for a mutex role, and directly holds the other, this is permitted and constructible.\n- Remove any implicit negative-only constraints that, when combined, block the actual scenario realization.\n- After revision, rerun both the assertion and the corresponding run MutexPossibleDuringEmergency to confirm the model admits the required scenario.\n\n=== ASSUMPTIONS REVIEW ===\nNone - assumptions appear sound\n\n=== REQUIREMENT UPDATES ===\nNone - no requirement updates justified\n\n=== UPDATED USER QUESTIONS ===\nNone\n\n=== Q&A STATUS UPDATE ===\nNone - no status changes\n\n=== HANDOFF TO RE ===\nPriority 1 (Critical blockers): Refactor DelegationMutex and related facts to constructively enable Emergency-mode mutex relaxation via delegation, ensuring the required scenario is admitted and assertMutexAllowedDuringEmergency passes.\nPriority 2 (Important improvements): After repair, add/adjust explicit positive scenario predicates to confirm the model can realize all exceptional Emergency behaviors as required.\nPriority 3 (Optional enhancements): Review and document the constructive scenario in the requirements for future traceability and regression prevention.\n```",
