    "evaluator_feedback": "```\n=== NEXT ACTION DECISION ===\nDecision: refine/narrow fix\nRationale: The current fix direction (constructive enabling of mutex relaxation via delegation in Emergency) is correct, but the model is still not constructively realizing the required scenario. The issue is modeling-level: transitions or constructive permissions for delegation in Emergency remain too weak or incomplete.\n\n=== VERIFICATION STATUS ===\n- Syntax: OK\n- Baseline: Satisfiable\n- Combined Model: Satisfiable\n- Assertions: Counterexamples (assertMutexAllowedDuringEmergency fails)\n- Instances: Meaningful (for other properties), but missing required Emergency mutex scenario\n- Vacuity: None\n\n=== REPAIR INSTRUCTIONS ===\n- Focus on the constructive realization of Emergency-mode mutex relaxation. The DelegationMutex fact is permissive, but the model lacks mechanisms to actually generate/realize states where users hold both mutex roles via delegation in Emergency mode.\n- Actions:\n  1. Introduce a scenario-enabling predicate or transition in the model to ensure delegation activity can occur during Emergency mode. For example, add a fact or transition rule that explicitly allows (and does not block) the creation of delegations in Emergency.\n  2. Review the transition logic and ensure that, in Emergency mode, users can:\n      - Delegate roles to other users (including to create a mutex-role situation where at least one is via delegation).\n      - Hold both mutex roles simultaneously via a mix of direct and delegated assignment, as permitted by requirements.\n  3. Add a scenario predicate (or run command) that witnesses: in some Emergency state, a user holds both members of a mutex pair with at least one via delegation, and not both via direct assignment. Use this as a positive test case to drive constructive instantiation.\n  4. Double-check that delegatedRoles and directRoles are correctly computed in all states, and that delegation creation is not blocked by over-strict facts (such as requiring delegator to always have the role in all states).\n- Example: Add a predicate (and a run command) like MutexPossibleDuringEmergency as a positive scenario, and ensure run MutexPossibleDuringEmergency produces a satisfying instance.\n- If the model still fails to realize the scenario, further reduce constraints in the delegation creation/transition rules for Emergency mode, stepwise, until the required state is constructively achievable.\n\n=== ASSUMPTIONS REVIEW ===\nNone - assumptions appear sound\n\n=== REQUIREMENT UPDATES ===\nNone - no requirement updates justified\n\n=== UPDATED USER QUESTIONS ===\nNone\n\n=== Q&A STATUS UPDATE ===\nNone - no status changes\n\n=== HANDOFF TO RE ===\nPriority 1 (Critical blockers): \n- Refine the model to constructively enable and realize delegation activity in Emergency mode, ensuring that the required Emergency mutex relaxation scenario occurs in at least one instance (user holds both mutex roles with at least one via delegation).\nPriority 2 (Important improvements): \n- Add explicit positive scenario predicates and run commands to validate that Emergency-mode mutex relaxation is not merely permitted but realized.\nPriority 3 (Optional enhancements): \n- Consider reviewing other Emergency delegation/role transition behaviors for possible further edge-case scenario checks.\n```",
