a delegation remains active in s2, but the delegator does not hold the role in s2, the transition should be disallowed.\n\n3. Avoid repeating failed strategies:\n   - Do not simply relax or reword the existing facts; instead, focus on modeling the constructive transitions and persistent properties that enable the required behaviors and prevent assertion failures.\n\n=== ASSUMPTIONS REVIEW ===\nNone - assumptions appear sound\n\n=== REQUIREMENT UPDATES ===\nNone - no requirement updates justified\n\n=== UPDATED USER QUESTIONS ===\nNone\n\n=== Q&A STATUS UPDATE ===\nNone - no status changes\n\n=== HANDOFF TO RE ===\nPriority 1 (Critical blockers): \n- Constructively enable the Emergency-mode mutex relaxation scenario so the model can witness a user holding both mutex roles, with at least one via delegation.\n- Enforce the constraint that delegators must hold the delegated role in all states where the delegation is active.\nPriority 2 (Important improvements): \n- Review all delegation-related transitions to ensure persistent and constructive enforcement of all delegation constraints and Emergency-mode exceptions.\nPriority 3 (Optional enhancements): \n- After repairs, add negative and vacuity checks to confirm the fixes do not overconstrain or underconstrain the model.\n```",
