    "evaluator_feedback": "```\n=== NEXT ACTION DECISION ===\nDecision: refine/narrow fix\nRationale: The model is now overconstrained, resulting in total unsatisfiability for all positive scenarios and requirements despite all assertions passing. This indicates that the recent changes (especially constructive delegation and persistent role-holding) are too strong or interacting in an unintended way. The fix direction (enabling constructive Emergency delegation and mutex relaxation) is correct, but the constraints must be narrowed/relaxed to restore satisfiability.\n\n=== VERIFICATION STATUS ===\n- Syntax: OK\n- Baseline: Unsatisfiable\n- Combined Model: Unsatisfiable\n- Assertions: Pass (no counterexamples)\n- Instances: N/A (no satisfying instances found)\n- Vacuity: Possible (assertions pass vacuously due to global unsat)\n\n=== CONVERGENCE_RECOMMENDATION ===\nStatus: FALSE\nReasoning: No satisfying instances are found for any positive scenario or requirements predicate. Assertions pass only vacuously; critical behaviors are blocked. The model is overconstrained and does not reflect the intended requirement coverage.\n\n=== REPAIR INSTRUCTIONS ===\n- Focus on the interaction between:\n    - DelegatorPersistentRoleHolding fact\n    - ConstructiveEmergencyMutexDelegation fact\n    - DelegationMutex fact\n    - DelegationConstraints fact\n- Suspect: The DelegatorPersistentRoleHolding fact, as currently written, requires that the delegator always holds the role in every state where the delegation is active. Combined with Emergency-mode delegation transitions and mutex logic, this may create impossible cycles (especially if delegator must always retain the role, even when the role is delegated away or when revocation occurs).\n- Action:\n    1. Carefully relax the DelegatorPersistentRoleHolding fact to allow the delegator to relinquish (or have temporarily revoked) the role in certain Emergency delegation transitions, as long as the requirements do not forbid this. Alternatively, ensure that the model permits the delegator to retain the role without blocking necessary delegation transitions.\n    2. In ConstructiveEmergencyMutexDelegation, ensure that the enabling of mutex role holding via delegation does not require impossible preconditions (e.g., requiring both the delegator and delegatee to simultaneously hold mutex roles in a way that cannot be reached).\n    3. Double-check that DelegationMutex and DelegationConstraints facts do not redundantly forbid Emergency scenarios that are supposed to be constructively enabled.\n    4. After each change, rerun only the previously unsatisfiable positive scenario predicates (e.g., MutexPossibleDuringEmergency, EmergencyDelegationRevocationScenario, PreEmergencyDelegationPersistenceScenario, EmergencyBypassFIFOScenario) to confirm that at least one meaningful instance is restored.\n    5. If relaxing the facts does not suffice, consider splitting or restructuring the facts so that Emergency-specific logic is only applied in Emergency mode, and that all delegation/role-holding cycles are avoidable.\n\n=== ASSUMPTIONS REVIEW ===\nNone - assumptions appear sound\n\n=== REQUIREMENT UPDATES ===\nNone - no requirement updates justified\n\n=== UPDATED USER QUESTIONS ===\nNone\n\n=== Q&A STATUS UPDATE ===\nNone - no status changes\n\n=== HANDOFF TO RE ===\nPriority 1 (Critical blockers): Relax and refactor DelegatorPersistentRoleHolding and ConstructiveEmergencyMutexDelegation facts to remove overconstraint blocking all positive scenarios; confirm at least one instance for key Emergency delegation/mutex scenarios is constructible.\nPriority 2 (Important improvements): Modularize Emergency-mode delegation logic to clearly separate it from Normal mode and avoid unintended cross-mode constraints.\nPriority 3 (Optional enhancements): Add additional positive witness predicates and negative test cases once model is stabilizing to ensure coverage and non-vacuity.\n```",
