    "is_reverted": false,
    "evaluator_feedback": "```\n=== NEXT ACTION DECISION ===\nDecision: refine/narrow fix\nRationale: The fix direction (enabling constructive Emergency-mode mutex relaxation via delegation) is correct, but the model remains too restrictive or insufficiently constructive to realize the required scenario; additional adjustments are needed to explicitly enable the transition and state where a user holds both mutex roles via delegation during Emergency.\n\n=== VERIFICATION STATUS ===\n- Syntax: OK\n- Baseline: Satisfiable\n- Combined Model: Satisfiable\n- Assertions: Counterexamples (assertMutexAllowedDuringEmergency fails)\n- Instances: Meaningful except for missing constructive Emergency mutex scenario\n- Vacuity: None\n\n=== REPAIR INSTRUCTIONS ===\nThe model needs an explicit constructive mechanism to realize the Emergency-mode mutex delegation scenario required by requirements and by assertMutexAllowedDuringEmergency.\n\n1. Add a transition predicate or fact to the model to explicitly enable, during Emergency mode, a state in which:\n   - At least one user directly holds a role r1,\n   - Another user (the delegator) delegates a mutually exclusive role r2 (where r2 in r1.mutexWith) to the same user during Emergency,\n   - The resulting state satisfies: the user holds both r1 and r2, with at least one via delegation, and never both via direct assignment.\n\n2. Suggested approach:\n   - Define a predicate (e.g., EmergencyMutexDelegationTransition) that, when Emergency mode is active, allows a user to receive a delegated mutex role from another user who currently holds it, even if the user already directly holds a conflicting mutex role.\n   - Ensure this predicate is invoked or integrated into the state transitions/facts for Emergency mode so that the scenario is guaranteed to be reachable.\n   - Example snippet (pseudocode, for illustration):\n     ```\n     pred EmergencyMutexDelegationTransition[s, s2: State] {\n       s.mode = Emergency and s2.mode = Emergency and Next[s, s2] and\n       some u: User, d: Delegation, r1, r2: Role |\n         r2 in r1.mutexWith and\n         r1 in directRoles[s, u] and\n         d.role = r2 and d.to = u and d in s2.activeDelegations and\n         not (r2 in directRoles[s, u])\n     }\n     ```\n   - Integrate this into the facts governing Emergency transitions or as a witness scenario for the assertion.\n\n3. Ensure that this mechanism does not allow both mutex roles to be held by direct assignment at any time (as already enforced), and that the revocation of Emergency delegations at Emergency exit is preserved.\n\n4. Re-run the assertion and scenario predicates to confirm the constructive scenario is now realized and the assertion passes.\n\n=== ASSUMPTIONS REVIEW ===\nNone - assumptions appear sound\n\n=== REQUIREMENT UPDATES ===\nNone - no requirement updates justified\n\n=== UPDATED USER QUESTIONS ===\nNone\n\n=== Q&A STATUS UPDATE ===\nNone - no status changes\n\n=== HANDOFF TO RE ===\nPriority 1 (Critical blockers): \n- Refine the model to constructively realize Emergency-mode mutex delegation: add a transition or fact that ensures a user can acquire both mutex roles (one via delegation) during Emergency, satisfying assertMutexAllowedDuringEmergency.\nPriority 2 (Important improvements): \n- Ensure Emergency delegation and revocation behaviors remain stable after changes.\n- Double-check that no other transitions or facts inadvertently block the required constructive scenario.\nPriority 3 (Optional enhancements): \n- Strengthen scenario predicates and add negative/vacuity checks for edge case coverage after primary fix is verified.\n```",
