    "evaluator_feedback": "```\n[DIAGNOSIS]:\nThe error \"The name 'wasDirect' cannot be found\" at line 161 occurs because Alloy 6 uses a single let-binding scope for each let statement; the let-bindings for wasDirect and nowDirect are defined in separate statements and thus are not both in scope for subsequent expressions. This means that in line 161, when the expression (wasDirect and nowDirect) is encountered, Alloy cannot resolve 'wasDirect' and 'nowDirect' as they are not in scope simultaneously.\n\n[FIX INTENT]:\nCombine the 'let' bindings for 'wasDirect' and 'nowDirect' into a single 'let' statement so both are in scope for all subsequent expressions within the block.\n\n[REPAIR INSTRUCTIONS]:\n- Step 1: Replace the two separate let-statements:\n    let wasDirect = r in directRoles[s, u] |\n    let nowDirect = r in directRoles[s2, u] |\n  with a single, joint let-binding:\n    let wasDirect = r in directRoles[s, u], nowDirect = r in directRoles[s2, u] |\n- Step 2: Check that all occurrences of 'wasDirect' and 'nowDirect' within the block now refer to this joint scope.\n\n[RATIONALE]:\nThis fix is Alloy 6 compliant because it ensures both variables are in scope for all subsequent expressions, resolving the scoping error without changing the logic. It is different from failed approaches because it addresses the root cause—scoping rules for let-bindings in Alloy 6—rather than syntax or function definition issues.\n\n[LESSON]:\nIn Alloy 6, when multiple let-bound variables are needed together, use a single let-statement with multiple bindings; otherwise, only the innermost let-binding is in scope at each expression.\n```",
