    "evaluator_feedback": "```\n[DIAGNOSIS]:\nThe error \"'The name \"wasDirect\" cannot be found.'\" at line 160 in fact DirectRoleAssignmentTransition is caused by the scoping rules of Alloy 6: variables introduced via let-expressions are only in scope for the single following expression (or block). In the code, wasDirect and nowDirect are introduced in a let-binding at line 151, but then are referenced in multiple separate top-level expressions within the body of the enclosing block, which is not allowed in Alloy 6. Unlike Alloy 4, Alloy 6 does not allow variables introduced in a let-binding to be visible across multiple sibling expressions inside a block.\n\n[FIX INTENT]:\nMove the let-binding for wasDirect and nowDirect inside each expression where they are directly used, so that the variables are always in scope where referenced.\n\n[REPAIR INSTRUCTIONS]:\n- Step 1: In fact DirectRoleAssignmentTransition, remove the let-binding for wasDirect and nowDirect from line 151 so they are not declared at the top of the block.\n- Step 2: Instead, in each of the conditional expressions that use wasDirect and/or nowDirect (i.e., lines 153 and 160), insert a new let-binding at the start of each such sub-block, so that wasDirect and nowDirect are declared freshly for each expression.\n  - For example, rewrite:\n    ```\n    (not wasDirect and nowDirect) => (\n        leqClearance[...] and\n        ...\n    )\n    ```\n    as:\n    ```\n    let wasDirect = r in directRoles[s, u], nowDirect = r in directRoles[s2, u] |\n      (not wasDirect and nowDirect) => (\n        leqClearance[...] and\n        ...\n      )\n    ```\n  - Do the same for the (wasDirect and nowDirect) expression.\n- Step 3: Ensure that wasDirect and nowDirect are only referenced within the scope of their respective let-expressions.\n\n[RATIONALE]:\nThis fix complies with Alloy 6's scoping rules by ensuring that let-bound variables are only referenced within the scope of their let-expression, and it differs from prior failed attempts that tried to combine the let-bindings at the top level.\n\n[LESSON]:\nIn Alloy 6, let-bound variables do not persist across multiple sibling expressions within a block; always use let-bindings inside each sub-block or expression that needs them.\n```",
