    "evaluator_feedback": "```\n[DIAGNOSIS]:\nThe syntax error at line 337, column 15 arises because Alloy 6 does not support the \"else\" keyword as a conditional operator at the top level of a fact or predicate body. In Alloy, the \"=>\" (implies) operator is used for conditionals, but \"else\" is only valid within certain expressions (not as an if-then-else construct spanning multiple statements/blocks). Here, the fact EmergencyProcessingPriority tries to branch: it uses \"no pending => ...\" then \"else ...\", which is not valid Alloy 6 syntax. The entire conditional logic needs to be written as a single Boolean formula, not as a branching block.\n\n[FIX INTENT]:\nRewrite the fact so that the conditional is expressed as a single Boolean formula using Alloy's logical operators, eliminating the erroneous use of \"else\".\n\n[REPAIR INSTRUCTIONS]:\n- Step 1: Remove the \"else\" keyword at line 337.\n- Step 2: Replace the \"if-then-else\" style branching with a single logical formula: use Alloy's \"or\" operator to combine the two cases, each guarded by their respective condition.\n    - That is, restructure the fact so it reads: \n      (no pending and ...rest for no pending...) or (some pending and ...rest for pending...).\n- Step 3: Adjust indentation and parentheses as needed so that the entire body is a single Boolean expression, not a sequence of statements.\n\n[RATIONALE]:\nThis approach is compliant with Alloy 6's requirement that facts and predicates must be single Boolean expressions, not imperative-style conditionals; it is different from failed attempts because it removes \"else\" entirely and expresses the logic purely through Boolean connectives.\n\n[LESSON]:\nIn Alloy 6, top-level \"if-then-else\" constructs are not allowed; all conditional logic must be expressed with logical operators (and, or, =>, etc.), not branching keywords.\n```",
